# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.Python
build/
# Amaranth/nextpnr output dirs for the ecp5-test applets; regenerable in ~5s.
# Both spellings: two of these were tracked for months, and every path inside
# the .il/.json/.rpt files named the machine that ran the build.
*_build/
build_*/
develop-eggs/
dist/
downloads/
eggs/
.eggs/
# Anchored, unlike the rest of this block, and deliberately. Unanchored `lib/`
# matches a directory of that name at ANY depth, and the only thing in this
# repo that ships a lib/ is a Flutter app -- where lib/ is not a build output,
# it is the entire source tree. It silently untracked all of
# debris/code/cynthion-{workspace,app}-prototype/app/lib/ (34 files) and two
# files of the retired dashboard, in an archive whose whole purpose is that the
# history survives. The Python-packaging sense of "lib" is a top-level build
# directory, so say that.
/lib/
/lib64/
parts/
sdist/
var/
wheels/
*.egg-info/
.installed.cfg
*.egg

# Virtual environments
venv/
ENV/

# IDE
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo

# Temporary files
tmp/
*.log
.DS_Store

# Large regenerable sim waveforms (exceeds GitHub 100MB limit; regenerate from *.py)
# Simulation waveforms: written by the sim scripts on every run.
*.vcd

# Generated bitstreams from the bring-up gateware
ecp5-test/power_monitor/build/

# Generated bitstreams from the sideband/advertiser test gateware
ecp5-test/adv_speed/build*/
ecp5-test/adv_uart/build/
ecp5-test/sideband/build/

# Agent worktrees: transient checkouts, created and removed by the tooling.
# Their branches are merged into main; ten rescue/* tags anchor the commits.
.claude/worktrees/

# Third-party datasheets: freely fetchable from the vendor, several MB each, and not
# ours to redistribute. sources/README.md is the manifest with URLs.
#
# `sources/*.pdf` matched one level only, so a `sources/datasheets/` subdirectory slipped
# 13 MB of vendor PDFs past it and into a public repo (1cf7d67). Recursive now, and text
# extractions are covered too -- a pdftotext dump redistributes the whole document just as
# effectively as the PDF, and is derived anyway.
sources/**/*.pdf
sources/**/*.txt

# Cargo build output. Regenerable by `cargo build`, ~49 MB, and it was tracked once by
# accident -- a merge carried it back in because the branch that removed it never had it
# to delete. The firmware artifact that matters is the one `scripts/bram_patch.py` puts
# into a bitstream, not the tree that produced it.
firmware/*/target/

# CPU matrix build output. Yosys netlists, nextpnr artifacts and the bitstreams
# they produce, regenerated by debris/scripts/cpu_matrix.py. The results that
# mattered are the area table in docs/linux-on-cynthion.md; these are 571M of
# intermediates and were tracked by mistake. Sources (VexiiRiscv.v, *_top.v),
# build scripts (*.ys, *.lpf) and reports (top.rpt, top.tim) stay tracked.
ecp5-test/riscv/matrix/*/vexii.json
ecp5-test/riscv/matrix/*/fmax.json
ecp5-test/riscv/matrix/*/fmax.cfg
ecp5-test/riscv/matrix/*/top.json
ecp5-test/riscv/matrix/*/top.il
ecp5-test/riscv/matrix/*/top.config
ecp5-test/riscv/matrix/*/top.bit
ecp5-test/riscv/matrix/*/top.svf
ecp5-test/riscv/matrix/*/top.debug.v
ecp5-test/riscv/matrix/*/build_top.*
